WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.300
I'm serious, people.

2
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:19.900
This video is not some false promise or a video full of advice you won't be able to use or that you've already heard somewhere.

3
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:29.300
In it I'd like to share five tips on how to learn almost any foreign language to a conversational level in just six months,

4
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:34.200
and at the same time make the process of learning a language much more interesting.

5
00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:24.900
physics, chemistry, bookkeeping, anything that had some connection with numbers,

6
00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:28.200
because I simply never understood numbers.

7
00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:31.600
I didn't like math teachers either, whatever they were like,

8
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:37.900
and somehow I didn't get the point of why I was learning this when I knew I would never work with it.

9
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:41.500
But then something simply happened.

10
00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:45.700
I noticed that English was going quite well for me.

11
00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:55.700
I simply remembered the words I read, remembered phrases, grammatical constructions and absorbed new information without much effort.

12
00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:07.800
All this, together with years of watching American films and series, resulted in my being able to communicate normally in this language already by the end of primary school.

13
00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:13.100
And after that I wanted to learn some new language, but I didn't know which.

14
00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:17.500
Italian, Russian, Greek, everything somehow seemed interesting to me.

15
00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:24.500
And so, thinking about it, I found a Spanish book somewhere in the house and started flipping through it.

16
00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:34.600
Soon I realized that I could understand quite a lot and that it would be interesting to learn a language like this, and the rest is history.

17
00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:42.000
From that moment I learned four foreign languages: Spanish, Portuguese, Italian and Slovak.

18
00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:50.700
Portuguese in some eight to ten months, and I brought Italian to a conversational level in just four to six months.

19
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:58.900
You're wondering how? By following these five steps that I'm about to show you, and it all begins with step number one.

20
00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:05.500
Step number one. Every day learn three nouns, three verbs and one adjective.

21
00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:17.800
Since I'm someone who has been learning foreign languages for years, I wanted to find a system that I could practice every day without it creating too big an obligation.

22
00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:26.900
I tried various methods, combining grammar and vocabulary, studying in the morning, in the evening, in longer or shorter periods.

23
00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:31.500
However, one way of learning proved to be very effective for me,

24
00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:35.200
and so I want to share it with you who are watching this.

25
00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:39.400
What I do when I start learning a language is that every day,

26
00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:45.100
except Sunday, I learn three nouns, three verbs and one adjective.

27
00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:49.200
You can realistically do this in five minutes a day.

28
00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:52.900
Let's say that every day, except Sunday, we learn three nouns.

29
00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:55.900
On a weekly level that will be 18 nouns,

30
00:03:55.900 --> 00:04:02.300
on a monthly level a bit under 80, and on a yearly level about 900 nouns.

31
00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:12.100
If we add another 900 verbs and some adjectives, that's over 2000 words in a year, which is quite enough to communicate with someone.

32
00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:17.200
Although I don't like math, this is, you'll admit, pretty good math.

33
00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:25.900
So, we study six days a week, and on the seventh day we repeat all of this to somehow refresh it in our heads.

34
00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:30.300
Step two: use the Duolingo app.

35
00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:39.600
If someone asked me what's the fastest and cheapest way to learn a foreign language, I'd say it's Duolingo.

36
00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:51.400
Using Duolingo, after only six to eight months I gained enough vocabulary to talk about most of the topics I need in daily conversation.

37
00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:59.500
Okay, maybe I couldn't talk about the greenhouse effect, but I probably can't do that in my own language either.

38
00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:08.400
For those who don't know, Duolingo is a free app or program where you can learn almost all of the world's languages.

39
00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:15.800
It has a nicely illustrated interface, the exercises are adapted to lead you progressively to higher levels

40
00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:21.900
and they constantly slip some old words into new lessons so that you don't forget them.

41
00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:27.100
The exercises consist of translating from the language you're learning into English,

42
00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:32.500
of translating from English into the language you're learning, listening to audio recordings,

43
00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:39.200
writing out the things you hear so you can practice writing, and recording your speech.

44
00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:47.900
After each exercise you also get certain points with which you can buy various additional lessons

45
00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:55.300
and unlock new words within the program itself, which somehow motivates you even more to keep learning.

46
00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:07.000
And you know what else is great here? The fact that Duolingo has a very large community of people who are just as passionate as you about learning a foreign language.

47
00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:16.400
If I, let's say, am learning Korean and someone is learning Spanish, we can communicate together and help each other learn that language.

48
00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:24.800
The great thing is that I can learn the language when I want to. I don't have to learn it on Wednesdays and Fridays from seven to eight.

49
00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:31.600
Because maybe I don't feel like it then. Maybe I'm not in the mood, maybe I'm sad, maybe I have some other jobs to do.

50
00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:37.400
I just might not feel like studying on that day when I'd be studying at some course I attend.

51
00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:46.900
I have nothing against language courses, but here I can adjust the time for myself to learn the language on the day when it suits me to learn it, say now,

52
00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:50.500
and to learn those things that I feel like learning that day.

53
00:06:50.500 --> 00:07:03.300
Today I can learn about, say, fruit, vegetables, animals, climate, weather, anything else that is interesting to me that day, and the learning itself then becomes much more fun.

54
00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:07.200
Step three. Have a friend in that country.

55
00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:21.100
After I learned the basic words of that language and went through most of the lessons on Duolingo, the next step would be to find someone who is a native of that language and try to communicate with them.

56
00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:32.000
If, say, you're learning some exotic language, like my Portuguese, look for those people on the internet, precisely on sites such as Duolingo.

57
00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:43.100
During 2013 a Brazilian named Nathan stayed in my apartment, with whom I then had the chance to speak Portuguese for the first time in my life.

58
00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:49.800
The moment came to form everything I had learned earlier inside myself into living words.

59
00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:55.000
And to be honest, people, the first couple of minutes were extremely hard.

60
00:07:55.000 --> 00:08:13.400
I had never talked with anyone in that language, except when I gave myself topics and tried to answer them, but after about ten minutes the brain somehow gets used to it, and words I didn't even know I knew start coming out of my mouth.

61
00:08:57.500 --> 00:09:13.900
In my opinion, one of the things people get most wrong when learning foreign languages is that they sign up for a course of two hours a week, which they sometimes skip, and then expect to make great progress through a few hours a month.

62
00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:19.000
Some studies say that for active knowledge of most European languages

63
00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:27.800
you usually need 600 to 800 hours of study, and at a pace of eight hours a week this can really take a long time.

64
00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:34.100
If you want to learn a language, you need to be in contact with it every day.

65
00:09:34.700 --> 00:09:40.700
Watch as many films as possible in that language, listen to as much music as possible, as many audiobooks as possible.

66
00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:46.500
Every seven days find two or three songs you like in that language,

67
00:09:46.900 --> 00:09:53.400
find the lyrics of those songs, pull out the words you didn't know before and translate the songs themselves.

68
00:09:53.900 --> 00:10:01.200
Is there anything nicer than knowing what a song you like is about in the language you're now learning?

69
00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:06.900
This is how you expand your vocabulary in a very spontaneous and fun way.

70
00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:20.600
While I was learning Spanish and Portuguese, I had over 300-400 songs from these countries on my phone that I constantly listened to when I went to meetings, workouts or for coffee with friends.

71
00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:30.600
Every day I listened to these songs so that I'd simply have as much of the language in my head as possible, and over time I'd simply learn some words without being aware of it.

72
00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:37.400
And to this day, when I go somewhere, I usually listen to some podcast or something in Italian, Spanish, Portuguese,

73
00:10:37.500 --> 00:10:45.700
and if you look at my Spotify list even today, you'll see that there are a lot of Latino and other songs there.

74
00:10:48.700 --> 00:10:53.400
Read newspapers from that country even if you don't understand a single word.

75
00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:59.800
The more foreign newspapers you read, the more you'll surely notice how some words repeat,

76
00:10:59.800 --> 00:11:02.900
and you'll absorb those words unconsciously.

77
00:11:03.700 --> 00:11:08.500
You'll also notice individual words that may be similar in another language,

78
00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:10.800
and so you'll remember them through that.

79
00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:13.200
Read as much as possible.

80
00:11:13.900 --> 00:11:17.600
Choose one text and try to analyze it in detail,

81
00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:19.700
and to extract the meaning from it.

82
00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:25.100
Maybe it seems exhausting to you, but believe me, it gives excellent results.

83
00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:28.700
Step five. It doesn't matter whether I make mistakes,

84
00:11:28.700 --> 00:11:30.000
what matters is that I try.

85
00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:33.300
What I see in many people who learn a language,

86
00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:37.300
even those who speak it, is a fear of speaking up.

87
00:11:37.900 --> 00:11:40.700
Many are afraid to speak in front of someone because they fear

88
00:11:40.700 --> 00:11:43.700
that a certain verb won't be in the right tense

89
00:11:43.700 --> 00:11:46.600
or that they don't know how a word is said.

90
00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:51.000
Imagine yourself in a position where someone comes to you who is learning our language

91
00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:52.800
and tries to speak it.

92
00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:56.200
And so he gets every case that exists wrong

93
00:11:56.200 --> 00:12:00.500
and makes a million mistakes, you surely won't laugh at him.

94
00:12:01.300 --> 00:12:02.500
He'll be likeable to you.

95
00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:07.900
You'll appreciate his effort, his persistence and his courage for trying.

96
00:12:08.500 --> 00:12:10.300
It's the same with you.

97
00:12:10.700 --> 00:12:14.900
Nobody will ever say how badly you learned a language

98
00:12:14.900 --> 00:12:18.500
or how you wrongly conjugated this verb in the preterite,

99
00:12:18.600 --> 00:12:24.700
but they will appreciate your effort and work to try to communicate in their language at all.

100
00:12:24.700 --> 00:12:32.800
Free yourself from this fear that somehow prevents you from moving from passive to active knowledge of a foreign language.

101
00:12:33.100 --> 00:12:41.400
It's enough to learn a certain stock of words so that you can communicate with those people, and now you already know how to do that.

102
00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:54.100
Now, people, since you've been watching this video for a long time, I'd like to share one bonus tip with you.

103
00:12:54.100 --> 00:13:02.200
Something that I'd say is the most important thing when it comes to learning foreign languages, and which is honestly mentioned quite rarely.

104
00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:12.400
So, the main tip for learning foreign languages is persistence and patience.

105
00:13:13.100 --> 00:13:22.500
Maybe this isn't something you expected, maybe it's nothing revolutionary, but if you think about it, learning foreign languages really comes down to that.

106
00:13:22.500 --> 00:13:33.300
If, for example, you go to the gym and on the first day you do 100 push-ups and, say, 50 sit-ups, probably nothing will happen.

107
00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:48.700
If you do that on the second, third, fifth day, probably again nothing will happen, but if every day or every other day you do the same amount of certain exercises, already in the fifth or sixth month you'll notice great results.

108
00:13:49.300 --> 00:13:55.300
It's the same thing here. If every day we set aside a little time, five, ten, fifteen, twenty minutes,

109
00:13:55.500 --> 00:14:05.400
to learn a foreign language, somehow imperceptibly we'll reach the stage where after a few months we're already speaking it fluently.

110
00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:13.800
And let learning foreign languages be fun and a process that you will like and that you won't do just because you have to.

111
00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:20.300
My mother kept telling me: learn German, son, German will be useful to you sometime in life,

112
00:14:20.300 --> 00:14:27.300
but I honestly hate German. I have nothing against those people, but I don't like the language at all,

113
00:14:27.300 --> 00:14:32.800
and if I were to learn it, it simply wouldn't be fun, for me personally,

114
00:14:32.800 --> 00:14:41.200
and I probably wouldn't listen to German music, watch German films and do similar things connected with it.

115
00:14:41.900 --> 00:14:52.800
Learn a language because it gives you something new, gives you the chance to learn about a country, another people, and to simply educate yourself further.

116
00:14:53.500 --> 00:15:02.600
Write me comments if you have other tips on how to learn a foreign language, and we'll see each other, people, in some new travel story.
